  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "The value of \\(x\\) at the intersection of \\(y=\\dfrac8{x^2+4}\\) and \\(x+y=2\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2+\\sqrt5\\)",
    "B": "\\(-2-\\sqrt5\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "At an intersection, both equations hold, so use \\(y=2-x\\) to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n\\frac8{x^2+4}=2-x.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\), the denominator \\(x^2+4\\) is positive. Multiplying by it is therefore valid and gives\n\\[\n8=(2-x)(x^2+4).\n\\]\nExpanding and rearranging, then factoring out \\(x\\), gives\n\\[\n0=x^3-2x^2+4x=x(x^2-2x+4).\n\\]\nTo see whether the second factor can vanish, complete its square:\n\\[\nx^2-2x+4=(x-1)^2+3>0.\n\\]\nThus the product can be zero only when \\(x=0\\). This gives \\(y=2\\), which satisfies both original equations. The unique real intersection therefore has \\(x=0\\), choice C.",
